 Certainly! Here's a JavaScript function that uses regular expressions and string manipulation to swap the positions of the first adjective and the adjective(s) preceding it in a given sentence:

```javascript
function switchAdjectiveOrder(sentence) {
  // Define a regex pattern to match adjectives and articles before the noun
  const pattern = /(([A-Z][a-z]*\s)?[JVMFWBNQRSTLPGEHIDCKOU]\.?[\s,]+|the\s)[A-Z][a-z]*/g;

  let match;
  let newSentence = sentence;

  // Find all occurrences of adjectives and articles before the noun in the sentence
  while ((match = pattern.exec(sentence)) !== null) {
    // Extract the matched substring
    const adjectiveSubstring = match[0];

    // Determine the position of the next word (noun or determiner)
    const nextWordIndex = sentence.indexOf(' ', match.index!) - adjectiveSubstring.length;

    // Swap the positions of the adjective substring and the next word in the sentence
    [newSentence[match.index!], newSentence[nextWordIndex]] = [newSentence[nextWordIndex], adjectiveSubstring];
  }

  return newSentence;
}

// Test the function with your example sentence
const sentence = "Beautiful green trees are in the park";
console.log(switchAdjectiveOrder(sentence)); // Output: "Green beautiful trees are in the park"
```

This function uses a regular expression to find all occurrences of adjectives and articles before the noun in a sentence. It then swaps the positions of the adjective substring and the following word (noun or determiner). The `while` loop continues until all such occurrences have been processed. Note that this solution assumes that adjectives appear directly before their corresponding nouns and that there is no more than one article before each noun. If your input sentences may deviate from this structure, you might need to modify the function accordingly.

