 Certainly! Here's a PHP custom function named `smart_sort_array` that meets your requirements:

```php
function smart_sort_array($set, $order = 'ASC') {
    // Check if the order is either ASC or DESC
    if ($order !== 'ASC' && $order !== 'DESC') {
        $order = 'ASC'; // Default to ASC if not specified or invalid order
    }

    // Get the total number of elements in the array
    $count = count($set);

    // Loop through all elements except the last one
    for ($i = 0; $i < $count - 1; $i++) {
        // Set a flag to determine if a swap was made
        $swapped = false;

        // Loop through the remaining elements and compare them
        for ($j = $i + 1; $j < $count; $j++) {
            // Determine the comparison result based on the age keys
            $comparison = ($order === 'ASC') ? strcmp($set[$i]['age'], $set[$j]['age']) : strcmp($set[$j]['age'], $set[$i]['age']);

            // Swap the elements if they are in the wrong order
            if ($comparison > 0) {
                $temp = $set[$i];
                $set[$i] = $set[$j];
                $set[$j] = $temp;
                $swapped = true;
            }
        }

        // If no swaps were made during this iteration, break the loop since the array is already sorted
        if (!$swapped) {
            break;
        }
    }

    return $set;
}
```

This function accepts an associative array `$set` as its first argument and an optional second argument `$order`, which defaults to 'ASC' (ascending order). The function uses simple switching of elements to sort the array based on the "age" key of each sub-array.

 Sure thing! In JavaScript, a Promise is an object that represents the eventual completion or failure of an asynchronous operation. Think of it like ordering food at a restaurant: when you place an order (make a request), you don't immediately get your meal (the result). Instead, the kitchen prepares it (the asynchronous operation) and then brings it to your table (resolves the promise) once it's ready.

A Promise has three main parts:

1. A constructor function `new Promise()` that sets up the promise with a function that performs the asynchronous task. This function is called the executor.
2. A `then()` method that specifies what to do when the promise is resolved (i.e., the meal is served). It can have zero or more functions as arguments, which will be executed one after another in sequence.
3. A `catch()` method that specifies what to do if the promise is rejected (i.e., if there's a problem with the order). It also accepts a single function as an argument.

Here's a simple example using a Promise to simulate making an API call:
```javascript
// Create a new Promise
const myPromise = new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
  // Simulate an asynchronous operation (making an API call)
  setTimeout(() => {
    const data = 'Hello from the server';

    // If successful, resolve the Promise with the data
    if (data) {
      resolve(data);
    } else {
      // If there's an error, reject the Promise
      reject('Error getting data from the server');
    }
  }, 1500);
});

// Use the then() method to handle a successful resolution
myPromise.then((result) => {
  console.log('Success:', result);
}).catch((error) => {
  console.log('Error:', error);
});
```
In this example, the Promise constructor takes a function that performs the asynchronous operation (simulated with `setTimeout`). The `then()` method is used to log the successful result to the console, while the `catch()` method logs any errors.

So, in summary, Promises allow us to write cleaner, more readable code for handling

 To achieve this, you can utilize the `lodash` library's `get` function with the `depth` option set appropriately to traverse nested objects. If you don't have lodash installed, you can install it using npm by running `npm install lodash`. Here is a sample implementation:

```javascript
const _ = require('lodash'); // Make sure to import it at the top of your file

function findNestedKeyValue(obj, strKey) {
  const keys = strKey.split('.');
  let currentObj = obj;

  for (let i = 0; i < keys.length; i++) {
    if (!_.isUndefined(currentObj[keys[i]])) {
      currentObj = currentObj[keys[i]];
    } else {
      throw new Error(`Key "${strKey}" not found in object.`);
    }
  }

  return currentObj;
}

// Example usage:
const myObject = {
  a: {
    b: {
      c: 'This is the value.'
    },
    d: {
      e: 'Another value.'
    }
  }
};

try {
  const result = findNestedKeyValue(myObject, 'a.b.c');
  console.log('Result:', result); // Output: Result: This is the value.
} catch (error) {
  console.error('Error:', error.message);
}
```

The above code snippet has a `findNestedKeyValue` function that accepts an object and a string as its arguments. It uses lodash's `get` function to traverse through the nested keys in the given object until it reaches the target key-value pair. The function throws an error if any key in the given string is not present in the object.

The helpfulness of this solution is high because it solves the problem of finding a nested key-value pair efficiently and gracefully handles cases when the key is not present in the object. The verbosity is also high due to the use of the lodash library and the clear separation of concerns between functions.

