 I'd be happy to help you write a React utility function for removing widows in text rendering! Widows refer to the last line of a paragraph that is incomplete, with only one or a few words extending to the next line. Eliminating widows can improve the visual appeal and readability of your text. Here's how you can create a custom React component or higher-order component (HOC) to address this issue:

**Option 1: Creating a Custom Component (LineComponent)**

First, let's create a new functional component called `LineComponent`:

```jsx
import React from 'react';

const LineComponent = ({ children }) => {
  const textNodes = React.useMemo(() => {
    if (!children || typeof children !== 'string') return [];
    const lines = children.trim().split('\n');
    return lines.map((line, index) => {
      return <span key={index}>{line}</span>;
    });
  }, [children]);

  return <>{textNodes}</>;
};

export default LineComponent;
```

Use this `LineComponent` as a wrapper for your text elements in the parent component:

```jsx
import React from 'react';
import LineComponent from './LineComponent'; // import it from where you saved it

function MyParagraph({ children }) {
  return <p><LineComponent>{children}</LineComponent></p>;
}

export default MyParagraph;
```

Now, use the `MyParagraph` component instead of the regular `p` tag:

