2. The Battle of the Marne (September 6-12, 1914, Marne River, France): The Battle of the Marne marked a turning point in the war, preventing the German advance towards Paris and forcing them into a stalemate. Allied forces under French General Joffre and British General Haig halted the German offensive, inflicting heavy casualties upon the Germans. A lesser-known factor contributing to the outcome of the battle was the use of taxis to transport French troops from the railhead to the front lines more quickly than the Germans could.

3. The United States Enters the War (April 6, 1917): Initially neutral, the US entered the war after Germany resumed unrestricted submarine warfare, sinking American merchant ships. The U.S. Congress declared war on Germany following the Zimmerman Telegram, an intercepted message revealing a proposed alliance between Mexico and Germany to attack the U.S. The entry of the United States significantly increased the manpower and resources available to the Allies, contributing to their ultimate victory.

